immune (e.g., hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis): considered but ranked lower without specific markers", "Neoplastic visceral mass: possible but less likely than hematological causes given lab findings", "Endocrine crisis (e.g., diabetic ketoacidosis): ruled out by normal glucose and lack of metabolic acidosis"], "alarm_features": ["Constitutional symptoms (weight loss, fever, night sweats)", "Pancytopenia with blasts (high suspicion for leukemia)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.7242921013412816, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["High peripheral blast percentage with pancytopenia is a hallmark of myeloid neoplasms like AML or CML blast crisis, providing moderate support for this categor…"], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute myeloid leukemia", "myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts", "chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis"]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.076, "danger": 0.85, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Pancytopenia with blasts in peripheral blood is inconsistent with lymphoid neoplasms (e.g., ALL), which typically present with lymphocytosis, not blasts or pan…"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.28542009884678743, "posterior": 0.11177347242921011, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Subacute presentation without organomegaly is less typical for chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms in accelerated phase, but does not definitively exclude the…"], "evidence_against": ["Constitutional symptoms like weight loss and fever are more characteristic of myeloid neoplasms than typical chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms, moderately r…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.65, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "time_course", "representative_diseases": ["chronic myeloid leukemia", "polycythemia vera", "essential thrombocythemia", "primary myelofibrosis"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.054, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Blasts in peripheral blood are not a feature of plasma cell disorders like multiple myeloma, providing weak evidence against B4."]}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.034, "danger": 0.2, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.34308573221429134, "posterior": 0.34308573221429134, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Chronic Myeloid Leukemia in Blast Crisis", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.1143619107380971, "posterior": 0.1143619107380971, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "phase_transformation", "classification_axis": "time_course", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Therapy-Related AML", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.2668444583888932, "posterior": 0.2668444583888932, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "risk_context", "representative_diseases": []}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are lymphoid blast morphology criteria or lack of B- or T-cell lineage markers in peripheral blood consistent with lymphoid neoplasm?", "summary": "Absence of lymphoid lineage markers in peripheral blood with blasts suggests against lymphoid neoplasm (e.g., ALL), as blasts are more typical in myeloid disorders."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute clinical course (57k WBC with 35% blasts) and lack of progressive organomegaly support chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm in accelerated phase?", "summary": "Subacute presentation without organomegaly is less typical for chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms in accelerated phase, but does not definitively exclude them."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could the subacute time course (days) and absence of severe organomegaly or skin findings (e.g., acrocyanosis) argue against myeloid neoplasm in favor of a chronic myeloid process?", "summary": "The absence of organomegaly or skin findings (e.g., acrocyanosis) does not strongly argue against myeloid neoplasm, as these features can vary depending on phase and subtype."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of pancytopenia, high blast percentage, and absence of reactive lymphocyte features contradict lymphoid neoplasm with increased blasts?", "summary": "Pancytopenia with blasts in peripheral blood is inconsistent with lymphoid neoplasms (e.g., ALL), which typically present with lymphocytosis, not blasts or pancytopenia."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the absence of prior known chronic myeloid disorder and presence of constitutional symptoms (weight loss, fever) inconsistent with chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm?", "summary": "Constitutional symptoms like weight loss and fever are more characteristic of myeloid neoplasms than typical chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms, moderately reducing likelihood of B3."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of blasts in peripheral blood argue against plasma cell disorders like multiple myeloma as the primary differential?", "summary": "Blasts in peripheral blood are not a feature of plasma cell disorders like multiple myeloma, providing weak evidence against B4."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.724], ["B1.1", 0.343], ["B1.3", 0.267]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No immediate life-threatening instability detected.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading terminal branch (B1.1) posterior (0.343) <0.75. Dangerous alternatives B1.2 (CML blast crisis, posterior 0.114, danger 0.9) and B1.3 (MDS with excess blasts, posterior 0.267, danger 0.85) remain unresolved with significant diagnostic uncertainty."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.95, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.34308573221429134, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Acute myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Chronic myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Multiple myeloma'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.724}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia", "posterior": 0.343, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1.3", "B1.2", "B3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=71%, source=both]\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: acute myeloid leukemia, autosomal dominant inheritance, somatic mutation\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloid Leukemia in Blast Crisis:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormal/excessive bleeding, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Therapy-Related AML:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase) vs De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: acute myeloid leukemia, somatic mutation\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase) vs Chronic Myeloid Leukemia in Blast Crisis:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormal/excessive bleeding, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase) vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Therapy-Related AML:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nDe Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia vs Chronic Myeloid Leukemia in Blast Crisis:\n  Favours first:  acute myeloid leukemia, autosomal dominant inheritance\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormal/excessive bleeding, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues\n\nDe Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Therapy-Related AML:\n  Favours first:  autosomal dominant inheritance, somatic mutation\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nChronic Myeloid Leukemia in Blast Crisis vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Therapy-Related AML:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormal/excessive bleeding, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nRelated to Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, lymphoid leukemia (disease), lymphoma\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase): bone marrow cancer, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, not otherwise specified\n\nRelated to De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia: acute disease, acute leukemia of ambiguous lineage, acute myeloid leukemia with mutated npm1\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloid Leukemia in Blast Crisis: chronic myelogenous leukemia, bcr-abl1 positive\n\nRelated to Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Therapy-Related AML: hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, myelodysplastic syndrome, myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts-1\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  and night sweats → pain → Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  and night sweats → fatigue → Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts"}
